          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is called
            %strong gaunt
            if all its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            are in fact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+invariance"} invariant
            under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %p
            Every gaunt category is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+foundations"} set-level foundations
            but not in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+foundations"} higher foundations
            where univalent categories are usually considered. More generally, gaunt categories are precisely the categories that are both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            and univalent.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#in_setlevel_foundations In set-level foundations
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong gaunt category
              or
              %strong stiff category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
            %p
          %p Expanding out, this becomes
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong gaunt category
              or
              %strong stiff category
              is a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              such that the set of isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong B
              between every two objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in Ob(C)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \in Ob(C)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \cong B
              if and only if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B
            %p
          %h3#in_type_theory In type theory
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with some notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+type"} path type
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong gaunt category
              or
              %strong stiff category
              is a strict univalent category.
          %p Expanded out, this becomes
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong gaunt category
              or
              %strong stiff category
              is a category whose type of objects is 0-truncated and which additionally satisfies a condition called
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Rezk completeness
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi idtoiso
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} idtoiso : (a=b) \to (a \cong b)
            %p
              from the identity type between two objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              to the type of isomorphisms between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              is an equivalence of types.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+type+theory"} set-level type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            is a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              is a gaunt category.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+parallel+pair"} walking parallel pair
              is a gaunt category which is not a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category"} free category
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
              is a gaunt category.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotonic"} monotonic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+homomorphisms"} field homomorphisms
              is a gaunt category.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              of a group is (in set-level foundations) a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+category"} skeletal category
              that is not gaunt (unless the group is
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial
            %li
              The
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+isomorphism"} walking isomorphism
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              and more generally any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
              is gaunt.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_other_properties_of_categories Relation to other properties of categories
          %p
            Gaunt categories are necessarily
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal"} skeletal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin"} thin
            gaunt category is skeletal, and since a thin skeletal category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+category"} poset category
            a thin gaunt category is also a poset category.
          %p Note that a gaunt category need not be thin, since we may have parallel non-isomorphisms which are not equal. Similarly, a thin category need not be gaunt since we may have isomorphisms that aren’t the identity.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of a gaunt category is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          %h3#relation_to_complete_segal_spaces Relation to complete Segal spaces
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            under the inclusion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ↪
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \hookrightarrow \infty Grpd
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"} complete Segal space
            precisely if the category is gaunt. More discussion of this is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Segal%20space#InSetByNervesOfCategories"} Segal space – Examples – In Set
          %h3#in_higher_foundations In higher foundations
          %p
            As noted above, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+foundations"} higher foundations
            the gaunt categories are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+categories"} univalent categories
          %p In addition, we have
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+completion"} Rezk completion
              of a core-thin category is a gaunt category.
          %h2#gaunt_categories
            Gaunt
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
            and other
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+foundations"} higher foundations
            %em gaunt Segal type
            or
            %em
              gaunt
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            makes sense as well.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong gaunt Segal type
              or a
              %strong
                gaunt
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+type"} Segal type
              or
              = succeed "-precategory" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+complete"} Rezk complete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal"} skeletal
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+complete"} Rezk complete
          %h2#related_invariant_concepts Related invariant concepts
          %p
            To make sense of the definition of a gaunt category, we need to use equality of objects: For every isomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : a \simeq b
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : a = b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            equals the identity at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            by an isomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : a \simeq b
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations"} univalent foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent category
            is another way of saying that every isomorphism is an identity (and uniquely so).
          %p
            Alternatively, we could avoid the equality on objects by requiring only that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : a \cong a
            be equal to the identity at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin category
            %em equivalent
            to a gaunt one.
          %p#Incidentally
            Incidentally, we may view both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            and categories up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            as embedded in the type of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flagged+categories"} flagged categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flagged+category"} flagged category
            consists of a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon X \to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flagged+categories"} flagged categories
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalence
            onto the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            as those flagged categories where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (up to homotopy: an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"}> h-set
            ”): see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+an+atlas"} category with an atlas
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            of categories-up-to-equivalence and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            within the type of flagged categories is then exactly this type of core-thin categories. (This is the semantics in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gpd"} Gpd
            of the fact that in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+categories"} univalent categories
